{"citation": "Runway 01L/19R geometry (9,400 ft × 150 ft) and ALSF-2 approach lighting supporting Cat II/III precision approaches, per FlightAware airport data and FAA ALSF-2 system specifications.", "claim": "At 6 a.m. a United widebody breaks out of the clouds on the Runway 1L approach at Dulles, and the first thing its crew looks for is a 2,400-foot ladder of light—an ALSF-2 approach lighting system, the standard for the Category II/III precision approaches this runway is built to fly.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-01", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["evidence-curator::E-0094", "quantitative-analyst::E-0034", "quantitative-analyst::E-0041"], "footnote_id": "1", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "FlightAware live fetch (2026-07-27) confirms runway 9,400 ft × 150 ft, ALSF-2 at both 01L and 19R ends, and ILS/DME capability. E-0094 (evidence-curator, usable) directly confirms: 'ALSF-2 is a standard 2,400 ft high-intensity ALS with sequenced flashers for Cat II/III precision approaches' on this specific runway. E-0034 confirms runway dimensions from the same FlightAware source. E-0041 confirms ALSF-2 standard 2,400 ft extent from FAA ALSF-2 system description. Prior JSONL omitted E-0094, which is the record that directly ties Cat II/III to this specific runway.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA Policy Review of Solar Energy System Projects on Federally-Obligated Airports (published FAA criteria for evaluating glare near approach paths); FAA Procedures for Handling Airspace Matters, Ch. 6 (EMI to navigation/radar treated as a substantial adverse effect).", "claim": "Glare near approach lights is subject to published FAA review criteria; EMI to a NAVAID or radar is a recognized \"substantial adverse effect\" that gets studied, not assumed.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-02", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["contrarian::E-0022", "operations-analyst::E-0031"], "footnote_id": "2", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Body sentence corrected: removed 'Plume magnitude is largely a function of on-site power generation;' which was not supported by the cited sources (FAA plume tool discusses overall risk level, not generation-source dependence; that claim is supported by technology-scout synthesis E-0047, not by the footnote-cited sources). 'Glare near approach lights is subject to published FAA review criteria' is supported by E-0022 (FAA 2021 Policy on Solar Energy System Projects, which establishes the published FAA glare-hazard screening framework for structures near approach paths). 'EMI … substantial adverse effect' is supported by E-0031 (PHAM Ch. 6 Sec. 3, verbatim quote). Footnote [^2] updated to match the corrected sentence. Prior footnote overclaimed the FAA plume tool as the source for 'plume magnitude driven by on-site generation.'", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "\"Dulles Master Plan Update Sets Framework for Decades of Airport Development\" (MWAA, July 16, 2025); \"New Master Plan for Dulles Airport Envisions Growth to 90 Million Annual Passengers\" (fifth runway planned since the 1950s, in the 1985 master plan, FAA-approved with the fourth runway; 88-gate expansion from 130 to 218 gates by 2080–2090).", "claim": "On July 16, 2025, the MWAA Board reaffirmed a master plan envisioning growth toward roughly 90 million annual passengers, a fifth runway, and 88 new gates—a build-out planned in some form since the 1950s, carried in the 1985 master plan, and FAA-approved alongside the fourth runway.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-03", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["airline-commercial-strategist::E-0014", "chief-engineer::E-0079"], "footnote_id": "3", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Live fetch of ffxnow.com (2026-07-27) confirms: Board approval July 16, 2025; 90 million annual passengers target; fifth runway planned; current 130 gates, 218 gates by 2080-2090 = 88-gate expansion. E-0014 confirms board date July 16, 2025, 1950s/1985 master plan history, and FAA approval alongside fourth runway. E-0079 incorrectly extracted '24 more gates' — the underlying ffxnow.com article shows 88 additional gates (218 − 130); this was a source-integrity failure in the evidence brief corrected by live fetch.", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "\"Dulles Airport's 2024 Passenger Total Set New All-Time Record\" (27.25 million passengers, 2024 all-time record); \"Washington Dulles' 5 Largest Airlines by Market Share\" (United ≈70% of flights; next carrier ≈5%).", "claim": "Dulles set an all-time record of 27.25 million passengers in 2024, and United flies roughly 70% of its departures—a concentrated, growing hub with a single carrier whose network depends on this airfield.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-04", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["airline-commercial-strategist::E-0010", "airline-commercial-strategist::E-0009"], "footnote_id": "4", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0010 (ffxnow.com, contextual) quotes verbatim: 'just over 27.25 million passengers traveled through the airport in 2024, representing a new all-time record.' E-0009 (Simple Flying, contextual) had cited United at 68%/Delta at 4%; prior verification pass live-fetched the Simple Flying article and found 70.42%/5.12%. Draft corrected to 'roughly 70%' and footnote updated to '≈70%/≈5%'. E-0009 is retained in evidence_ids because it points to the correct underlying source (Simple Flying market-share article), though its extracted figures were stale.", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "14 CFR 77.19, Civil Airport Imaginary Surfaces (precision approach surface, 50:1 for the first 10,000 ft); analyst construction from the 50:1 slope: a 50-ft object reaches the surface ~2,500 ft from origin.", "claim": "Under 14 CFR 77.19, a precision runway's approach surface rises at just 50:1, so a 50-foot object reaches the surface only about 2,500 feet from its origin—and data-hall roofs with rooftop chillers and exhaust stacks routinely exceed 50 feet.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-05", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0035", "quantitative-analyst::E-0036"], "footnote_id": "5", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0035 (eCFR, usable, is_primary: true) quotes 14 CFR 77.19 verbatim: 'The approach surface extends for a horizontal distance of 10,000 feet at a slope of 50 to 1 … for all precision instrument runways.' Arithmetic confirmed in this remediation: a 50-ft object reaches the 50:1 surface at 50 × 50 = 2,500 ft from origin. E-0036 contains identical analyst construction. eCFR URL redirected to anti-bot gateway during live fetch attempt; regulatory text is established and E-0035 quote is verbatim. 'Data-hall roofs … routinely exceed 50 feet' is a design observation not requiring its own citation.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA AC 150/5300-13B, Airport Design (departure/takeoff clearance surface, 40:1): 25 ft of allowable object height at 1,000 ft off the departure end.", "claim": "The departure surface is tighter still, allowing only 25 feet of height 1,000 feet off the departure end.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-06", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0037"], "footnote_id": "6", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0037 (is_primary: true, contextual) cites AC 150/5300-13B Airport Design and TERPS 40:1 OCS. Arithmetic confirmed in this remediation: 1,000 ft ÷ 40 = 25 ft. AC 150/5300-13B not directly fetched; the 40:1 slope is established TERPS standard consistent with E-0037. For a formal regulatory filing, the exact table and section in the current AC should be cited.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "Analyst construction from standard 3-degree glidepath geometry (50 ft threshold crossing height): ≈102 ft AGL at 1,000 ft, ≈155 ft at 2,000 ft, ≈207 ft at 3,000 ft from the threshold.", "claim": "And whatever is built there—plume, glare, or structure—sits in the band where an aircraft on a standard 3-degree glidepath is barely 200 feet above the ground at 3,000 feet from the threshold.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-07", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0040"], "footnote_id": "7", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Arithmetic independently verified in this remediation: height at 3,000 ft = 50 (TCH) + 3,000 × tan(3°) = 50 + 3,000 × 0.05241 = 50 + 157.2 ≈ 207 ft AGL. 'Barely 200 feet' accurately characterizes 207 ft as just above 200 ft. E-0040 contains this exact calculation. Standard glidepath geometry is primary input to the disclosed calculation and was checked.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "14 CFR Part 77 (§77.9 notice via Form 7460-1; obstruction evaluation ending in a Determination of No Hazard or Hazard; $1,000/day civil penalty for knowing/willful failure to notify).", "claim": "Construction in an approach surface requires a Form 7460-1 filing and an FAA obstruction evaluation ending in a Determination of No Hazard or Hazard; skipping the notice carries a $1,000-per-day civil penalty.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-08", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["chief-engineer::E-0073", "chief-engineer::E-0072", "airport-coo::E-0058", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0082"], "footnote_id": "8", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0073 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes FAA Part 77 page verbatim: 'Persons who knowingly and willingly violate the notice requirements of part 77 are subject to a civil penalty of $1,000 per day until the notice is received.' FAA.gov Part 77 page returned HTTP 403 during live fetch. E-0058 (14 CFR Part 77, usable) and E-0082 (14 CFR Part 77, usable) corroborate the 7460-1 process and OE/AAA outcome. E-0072 (FAA Part 77 notice page, usable) corroborates the 45-day notice and 60-day study timeline.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA Airport Sponsor Assurances (Grant Assurances 19, 20, 21, 22, 29); 49 U.S.C. 47107(a)(9) (statutory basis for GA 20, protect terminal airspace) and 47107(a)(10) (statutory basis for GA 21, compatible land use).", "claim": "Grant Assurances 19, 20, 21, 22, and 29 bind MWAA, and 20 and 21 trace to statute—49 U.S.C. 47107(a)(9) and (10)—obligating the sponsor to protect terminal airspace and keep nearby land compatible.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-09", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["airport-coo::E-0059", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0085", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0086", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0087"], "footnote_id": "9", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0086 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes 49 U.S.C. 47107(a)(9) verbatim: 'appropriate action will be taken to ensure that terminal airspace required to protect instrument and visual operations … will be cleared and protected.' E-0087 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes 47107(a)(10) verbatim: 'appropriate action … to restrict the use of land next to or near the airport to uses that are compatible with normal airport operations.' E-0085 (is_primary: true, usable) confirms GA 20 → 47107(a)(9) mapping. E-0059 (is_primary: true, usable) confirms GA 19, 20, 21, 22, 29 text. FAA assurances page returned HTTP 403 during live fetch; four usable evidence records corroborate from the same primary authority.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "Section 163, FAA Reauthorization Act of 2018, limiting FAA authority over on-airport land use to development affecting (A) safe/efficient aircraft operation, (B) safety of persons/property on the ground, or (C) the value of prior federal investment.", "claim": "Frame the objection on the exact standard the FAA still applies after Section 163 of the 2018 Reauthorization Act narrowed its reach: whether development (A) materially impacts safe and efficient aircraft operation, (B) endangers people or property on the ground, or (C) diminishes the value of prior federal investment.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-10", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0083"], "footnote_id": "10", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0083 (contextual) quotes Section 163's three-prong framework from Kaplan Kirsch legal analysis: '(1) materially impact the safe and efficient operation of aircraft … (2) adversely affect the safety of people or property on the ground … (3) adversely affect the value of prior Federal investments to a significant extent.' E-0083 also flags that Section 743 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization Act revised Section 163; the effect on the (A)/(B)/(C) framework is not documented in the evidence ledger. Retained as verified with this caveat flagged for counsel review before use in a formal FAA submission.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "\"Dulles Airport Lands $41M Federal Grant for Concourse E Construction\"; FAA Airport Infrastructure Grant, FY2026 (~$41.8M + ~$22.1M ≈ $64M)—a documented floor for the prior-federal-investment interest Grant Assurance 29 and Section 163(C) protect.", "claim": "That last prong is not abstract; two 2026 federal grants alone put roughly $64 million into Dulles—a documented floor beneath the decades of AIP-funded airfield and NAVAID value at stake.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-11", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["quantitative-analyst::E-0043"], "footnote_id": "11", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Live fetch of ffxnow.com (2026-07-27) confirms $41.8M FAA Airport Terminal Program grant for Concourse E. E-0043 (contextual) quotes $22,121,621 AIG FY2026; arithmetic: $41.8M + $22.1M ≈ $63.9M ≈ $64M confirmed. Correction from prior pass: footnote removed erroneous '(C)' from 'Grant Assurance 29(C)' — GA 29 governs Airport Layout Plan compliance and has no (C) subpart; the prior-federal-investment prong is Section 163(C) only.", "verification_status": "corrected"}
{"citation": "FAA AC 150/5200-33C, Hazardous Wildlife Attractants On or Near Airports (current; supersedes 33B, which is cancelled), recommending 10,000 ft separation for turbine-aircraft airports and up to 5 statute miles where an attractant could move wildlife into approach/departure airspace; analyst construction placing a runway-end pond almost entirely inside the 10,000-ft standard.", "claim": "The stormwater pond compounds the problem: current guidance in AC 150/5200-33C recommends 10,000 feet of separation from turbine-aircraft movement areas, and a pond at the runway end sits almost entirely inside that standard.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-12", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["operations-analyst::E-0028", "operations-analyst::E-0029", "quantitative-analyst::E-0038", "quantitative-analyst::E-0039"], "footnote_id": "12", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0028 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes AC 150/5200-33C verbatim: '10,000 feet from airports serving turbine-powered aircraft.' E-0029 (is_primary: true, usable) confirms 33B is cancelled and 33C is current. E-0038 (is_primary: true, usable) confirms the 10,000 ft standard from the AC. E-0039 (contextual) calculates '~95% inside' using an assumed actual separation of 500 ft — not a surveyed distance from the site plan. Body and footnote qualified from '~95%' to 'almost entirely inside' to avoid asserting an unsurveyed specific percentage. The directional argument holds: any runway-end pond is dramatically inside the 10,000-ft standard regardless of whether the actual distance is 200 ft or 1,500 ft.", "verification_status": "qualified"}
{"citation": "\"The Airports Authority Announces Sale of 424 Acres of Western Lands at Dulles International\" (MWAA press release, 2018): $236.5 million; ~$558,000/acre; Digital Realty; \"Airport Land Sale Expected to Help Lower Dulles Ticket Prices\" (~$207 million earmarked to reduce airline rentals, fees, and charges).", "claim": "In 2018 it sold 424 acres of Dulles \"Western Lands\" to Digital Realty for $236.5 million—about $558,000 an acre—netting roughly $207 million earmarked to lower airline costs.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-13", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["infrastructure-economist::E-0001", "contrarian::E-0018", "contrarian::E-0019", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0090"], "footnote_id": "13", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Live fetch of MWAA press release (2026-07-27) confirms: 424 acres, $236.5 million, Digital Realty, proceeds earmarked for CPE reduction. Per-acre arithmetic: $236.5M ÷ 424 = $557,783 ≈ $558,000/acre confirmed. E-0018 (usable) confirms net ~$207M from Loudoun Now reporting. E-0019 and E-0090 corroborate from the primary MWAA press release. Disputed E-0068 (LandApp, '433 acres') is contradicted by the primary MWAA document; 424 acres is correct.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "\"Data Center Alley: Ashburn, VA\" (Ashburn/Loudoun estimated to carry ~70% of global internet traffic); ACI, \"Airport Non-Aeronautical Revenues\" (~46% of total U.S. airport revenue is non-aeronautical, per 2025 ACI-NA Concessions Benchmarking Survey).", "claim": "Ashburn next door carries an estimated 70% of global internet traffic; airport-adjacent, power-and-fiber-served land is among the most valuable non-aeronautical acreage in American aviation, and non-aeronautical revenue is already about 46% of U.S. airport income.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-14", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["contrarian::E-0024", "aviation-historian::E-0066"], "footnote_id": "14", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0024 (Lightyear.ai, contextual) quotes: 'an estimated 70% of global internet traffic passes through it.' Draft correctly uses 'estimated.' E-0066 (ACI blog, contextual) cites 2025 ACI-NA Concessions Benchmarking Survey for the 46% figure. Lightyear.ai returned HTTP 403 during live fetch; ACI blog not re-fetched in this pass. Both sources are consistently cited industry figures; qualifying language in the draft ('estimated,' 'about') is appropriate for contextual sources.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "FAA Airport Improvement Program Grant Assurances (GA 24, self-sustaining rate structure); FAA \"Thermal Exhaust Plume Impact on Airport Operations: Technical Guidance and Assessment Tool\" (overall flight-disruption risk rated low; significant mainly to light aircraft in calm/cold/unstable conditions).", "claim": "Grant Assurance 24 requires the airport to be as self-sustaining as possible—and on that reading this project *advances* the assurances the original letter invokes—while the FAA rates the overall plume flight-disruption risk as low, significant mainly to light aircraft in calm, cold, or unstable conditions—not to the transport-category jets that serve this airport.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-15", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["contrarian::E-0020", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0088"], "footnote_id": "15", "primary_source_checked": false, "retained": true, "verification_note": "E-0020 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes GA 24: 'Assurance 24 requires sponsors to set rates and charges to make the airport as self-sustaining as possible.' E-0088 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes FAA Technical Guidance: 'overall risk … low … significant turbulent effects … most critical during low altitude flight in calm and cold air.' The inference that transport-category jets face lower risk than light aircraft is logically sound from 'significant mainly to light aircraft' but is an inference, not a direct quote from the source. Body sentence restructured to merge the GA 24 attributed claim into the same sentence as [^15], ensuring the attribution has a footnote in the same sentence.", "verification_status": "qualified"}
{"citation": "\"The Airports Authority Announces Sale of 424 Acres of Western Lands at Dulles International\" (MWAA press release): Western Lands were part of an 854-acre tract acquired 2005–2007; surplus remaining after the fourth runway and support area were built.", "claim": "The Western Lands were the surplus that remained *after* the fourth runway and its support area were built.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-16", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["contrarian::E-0019", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0090"], "footnote_id": "16", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Live fetch of MWAA press release (2026-07-27) confirms: 'The Western Lands are part of an 854-acre tract acquired by the Airports Authority between 2005 and 2007 to construct a fourth runway and additional facilities. After building the runway and support area, the Western Lands portion of the property has remained undeveloped.' E-0019 quotes this verbatim. E-0090 corroborates from the same primary source.", "verification_status": "verified"}
{"citation": "MWAA History and Facts (federal lease under the Metropolitan Washington Airports Act of 1986, executed June 7, 1987, extended to 2100); \"DOT Secretary Sean Duffy Launches New Initiative to Revitalize Dulles Airport.\"", "claim": "The Authority operates Dulles under a federal lease running to 2100, under active federal pressure to modernize the airport.", "claim_id": "CLAIM-17", "draft_sha256": "3618531e4673e2754991da632b3399f9c8f2cafd72f38043c4cebc725c862d25", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0092", "regulatory-political-analyst::E-0093"], "footnote_id": "17", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "MWAA History and Facts page fetched (2026-07-27): confirms original lease executed June 7, 1987 under the Metropolitan Washington Airports Act of 1986. E-0092 (is_primary: true, usable) quotes the 2024 extension: 'in 2024, it was extended an additional 33 years … the Airports Authority's lease of the Airports will expire in 2100.' The 2100 expiry was not returned by the live page fetch (which showed only the original 50-year term), but E-0092 is marked usable and provides the specific extension language. E-0093 (is_primary: true, contextual) cites FAA newsroom for the DOT/Duffy modernization initiative. 'Active federal pressure' is qualified per E-0093's contextual status.", "verification_status": "verified"}
